  "worked_solution": "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. Moreover, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and every such $s,$ we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{n}f(t)}=(-1)^{n}{F}^{(n)}(s).\n$$\n\nIn particular, for $n=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nL{tf(t)}=-{F}^{\u2032}(s).\n$$\n\nWe have that $f(t)=\\operatorname{cosh}\u20617t.$ Using the given result with $\\omega =7,$ the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-49},\\,s>7.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{\u2032}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-49}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(s({s}^{2}-49)^{-1}) \\\\  & =({s}^{2}-49)^{-1}+s(-({s}^{2}-49)^{-2}\\cdot 2s) \\\\  & =\\frac{{s}^{2}-49}{({s}^{2}-49)^{2}}-\\frac{2{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}-49)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-49-{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}-49)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{s}^{2}+49}{({s}^{2}-49)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the smoothness property,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t\\operatorname{cosh}\u20617t}=-{F}^{\u2032}(s)=\\frac{{s}^{2}+49}{({s}^{2}-49)^{2}},\\,s>7.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
